  "result": "The 30-minute lesson on O3 is written, with a quiz ready behind it. I haven't opened the lesson in a browser or run any word-count or link check, so the length and links are unchecked.\n\n**Lesson:** `lessons/0001-context-and-memory-in-an-agent.html`\n\nIt covers:\n- the context window and what fills it\n- what is kept between steps: the program's message list, re-sent on every call\n- why that makes the window drive cost\n- what is kept between sessions: nothing unless the program saves it\n- retrieval over the manuals, and notes the agent writes itself\n\nIt ends on a worked example that places each piece of the makerspace assistant's information in the system prompt, the saved store, a tool, retrieval or the message list. It also has recall questions, nine practice checks, two figures and a \"your turn\" task.\n\n**Quiz:** quiz 0002 is ready, with its key in `keys/0002.json`. Ask me for it when you want to take it. Quiz 0001 from the agent-loop session is still ungraded.\n\n**Other files written:**\n- `cheatsheets/0001-context-and-memory.html`\n- `RESOURCES.md`, listing the Anthropic sources I cited\n- a note in `NOTES.md`\n\n**Caveats:**\n- **Illustrative numbers:** the lesson's cost example ($3 per million input tokens, 60,000-token manual) is invented and labelled that way. Check your provider's real pricing before you put numbers in the design review.\n- **No community link:** I couldn't verify one in this session, so I left it out and listed it as a gap in `RESOURCES.md`.\n\nQuestion for you: do you want the next lesson to cover when a fixed workflow beats an agent, or splitting work across several agents? I'd start with workflow versus agent.",
